## The interactive tool

Open `docs/index.html` in any browser, or visit the live GitHub Pages link above.

- **Size metric toggle**: switch between in-game (player-generated) and real-world
  passenger volume as the model's size variable.
- **Distance decay slider ($k$)**: continuously adjustable from 0 to 1.5. The gravity
  model and Markov chain steady-state are recomputed live in the browser at every value
  — nothing is pre-baked to a fixed set of $k$ values.
- **Centrality / Size panel**: ranked, scrollable lists of all 129 airports, updating
  live with the slider and toggle.
- Click any airport marker (or list row) to see its centrality rank, centrality score,
  passenger volume, and H3 market share.

No build step, no dependencies — it's a single self-contained HTML file.

## Running the analysis pipeline

```bash
cd analysis
pip install -r requirements.txt
python centralityPipeline.py
```

This reproduces the full pipeline described in the paper: loading the calibration and
airport data, computing the Haversine distance matrix, building the gravity-model
transition matrix, solving for the steady-state distribution via power iteration, and
running the sensitivity analysis across $k$.

## A note on data collection

All calibration figures were collected using AEn's in-game Route Research tool with
**route awareness, class reputation, and ticket price held at their default/100%
values** across every observation, so that the resulting "maximum capturable passenger
load" figures are directly comparable across routes. If you extend this dataset, keep
these settings standardized or your new observations won't be comparable to the
existing 60.

The tables and associated scripts in the folder 'JMP Tables and Scripts' were also used to carry out part of the analysis in this project. 

## Using this for your own airline

The framework is airline-agnostic — swap in your own airline's destination list,
coordinates, and a fresh calibration sample, and the same pipeline and tool will produce
a centrality ranking for your network. `data/H3_data_collection_template.xlsx` is set up
to make this straightforward: replace the airport list and re-collect the two data
columns (weekly pax + market share, and ~60 calibration routes), then re-run the
regression (JMP or any statistics package) to get new $C$, $\alpha_o$, $\alpha_d$
estimates, and update the constants at the top of `docs/index.html`.

